# Reproduce: bash scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, Docker 29.8.1; remote host container image chaos-verify:local (Debian 12, git, python3, ripgrep).
# Commit under test: the commit that adds these checks and this transcript together (previous HEAD 60e215fe). Tokens are 64-hex one-time credentials and are never printed by the script. The one-time credential of the forwarding server lives in /shared/tokens-fwd and is likewise never echoed.
# This run supersedes the 80-check one of the same lab. It adds local port forwarding, 22 checks: a python3 http.server on the remote host is fetched through a forward started from the other container and the digest compared against the file on that host's disk; a hand-written RFC 6455 service is reached the same way and returns a file that exists only on the remote host, unreadable from the developer machine, so a completed WebSocket session through the tunnel is observed rather than assumed; the operator's --allow-forward-to list is shown to decide the target (an unlisted target is refused although a server really is listening on that port, and a server asked to advertise port-forward with no target listed refuses to start, as are zero ceilings); the lifetime the server answers with is its own ceiling rather than the client's request; a spent grant closes its own local listener and the port is bindable again. One check leaves the net +22 rather than +23: `port-forward` is no longer in the list of capabilities the client refuses outright, because it is now implemented.


